DOMAIN: Historical Necessity, Teleology, Determinism, and Agency
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-10
PAPERS_FOUND: 16 total (High: 7, Medium: 6, Low: 3)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers the philosophical core of Kautsky's system: his conception of
historical necessity, the law-governed development of society toward socialism, and
the problem of reconciling causal determinism with human agency. Kautsky grounded his
Marxism in a thoroughgoing naturalism: history, like nature, unfolds according to
discoverable laws, and socialism's coming is as certain as a natural scientific
prediction. This position has been criticized from two directions — the "fatalism"
charge (Luxemburg, Bernstein) that it paralyzes revolutionary will by making
socialism seem automatic, and the "mechanism" charge (Lukács, Gramsci) that it
reduces society to economic-material causes without dialectical mediation. The neo-
Kantian challenge (Bernstein, Vorländer, Hermann Cohen, the Marburg school) pushed
for a sharp distinction between the scientific "is" and the ethical "ought," arguing
that Marxist determinism cannot ground moral imperatives, and that socialism requires
an independent ethical justification. Kautsky resisted these challenges, insisting
that ethics and historical necessity are compatible, but critics found this response
philosophically unsatisfactory.

G. A. Cohen's analytical reconstruction of Marx's theory of history (1978) formalized
the "technological determinist" reading, positing that productive forces tendentially
develop and determine the character of productive relations. Cohen later (1986) grappled
directly with how such historical inevitability is compatible with human agency. The
broader literature on teleology in historical materialism — from Honderich's analytic
critique to the historical survey in Truper, Chakrabarty, and Subrahmanyam (2015) —
situates Kautsky's positions in a wider philosophical landscape. Berlin's "Historical
Inevitability" (1954) remains the classic liberal-pluralist attack on all forms of
historical determinism, directly relevant as a foil. Suchting's analysis of Marx and
Popper distinguishes types of historical law and provides conceptual resources for
assessing Kautsky's claims.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
These debates — fatalism vs. voluntarism, scientific law vs. ethical norm, teleology
vs. contingency — define the philosophical stakes of Kautsky's philosophy of history
and explain why it attracted sustained criticism from neo-Kantians, revisionists, and
later Western Marxists. Understanding them is essential to assessing what is original
and what is problematic in Kautsky's systematic account.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Direct treatment of Kautsky's own texts on historical necessity (especially "The
Materialist Conception of History," 1927) remains scattered across secondary literature
rather than collected in dedicated philosophical monographs. The relationship between
Kautsky's determinism and contemporary debates in analytic philosophy of history is
underexplored.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Use Cohen (1979), Honderich (1982), and Suchting (1972) to frame the analytic
debate about what kind of determinism historical materialism espouses; use the
neo-Kantian literature (Widmer 2023, Beiser 2009, Bernstein 1993) to develop the
ethical challenge; use Blackledge (2006) and Gronow (2016) as the primary secondary
literature on Kautsky's specific historical theory.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Productive-forces determinism: 3 papers (Cohen 1979, Honderich 1982, Suchting 1972)
- Neo-Kantian ethical critique: 4 papers (Widmer 2023/2024, Beiser 2009, Bernstein 1993)
- Historical teleology/inevitability: 3 papers (Berlin 1955, Cohen 1986, Truper et al. 2015)
- Kautsky-specific secondary literature: 4 papers (Blackledge 2006, Gronow 2016, Steger 1997, Bogdanov/Rowley 2024)
- Kautsky's own texts: 2 entries
====================================================================
}

@book{cohen1979karl,
  author = {Cohen, G. A.},
  title = {Karl Marx's Theory of History},
  year = {1979},
  publisher = {Princeton University Press},
  doi = {10.1515/9780691213002},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Cohen reconstructs Marx's theory of history as a form of productive-forces determinism: the tendency of productive forces to develop explains the succession of modes of production, and the economic base explains the superstructure. This is the most rigorous analytic defense of the "technological determinist" reading of historical materialism, attributing to Marx a commitment to lawlike necessity in history.

  RELEVANCE: Establishes the philosophical benchmark against which Kautsky's own determinism must be assessed. Kautsky's reading of Marx (productive forces as the engine of history, socialism as historically necessitated) closely mirrors Cohen's reconstruction, and critics of "orthodox Marxism" often use Cohen's analysis as a proxy for the Second International position. Essential for understanding what philosophical commitments the fatalism and mechanism critiques are targeting.

  POSITION: Productive-forces determinism; analytic reconstruction of historical materialism.
  },
  keywords = {historical-materialism, productive-forces, determinism, analytic-Marxism, High},
  abstract = {First published in 1978, this book rapidly established itself as a classic of modern Marxism. In this expanded edition Cohen offers his own account of the history, and the further promise, of analytical Marxism. He also expresses reservations about traditional historical materialism, in the light of which he reconstructs the theory.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{cohen1986historical,
  author = {Cohen, G. A.},
  title = {Historical inevitability and human agency in {Marxism}},
  journal = {Proceedings of the Royal Society of London. A. Mathematical and Physical Sciences},
  year = {1986},
  volume = {407},
  number = {1832},
  pages = {65--87},
  doi = {10.1098/rspa.1986.0085},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Cohen directly addresses the tension between historical inevitability in Marxism and the exercise of human agency. He argues that inevitability and agency are compatible: socialism is inevitable not despite human action but partly through it, since people's choices are themselves causally shaped by material conditions that tend to produce revolutionary agency. The paper probes how determinism in the strong sense can coexist with meaningful political will.

  RELEVANCE: This is the central philosophical problem of Kautsky's position: if socialism is inevitable, what motivates political action? Cohen provides the most precise analytic treatment of this question. The "conditional inevitability" view — socialism comes if people do what they are causally disposed to do — maps closely onto Kautsky's own stance that historical laws do not remove but rather explain revolutionary will.

  POSITION: Compatibilist reading of Marxist historical inevitability.
  },
  keywords = {determinism, agency, inevitability, Marxism, historical-materialism, High},
  abstract = {Abstract Marx and Engels believed that the advent of a socialist society was inevitable. How was that belief compatible with their advocacy of political activity to bring about socialism? I reply that Marx and Engels thought socialism was inevitable, not whatever people might do, but because of what people, being rational, were bound, predictably, to do. It is therefore no more irrational for Marxists to struggle for the goal they regard as inevitable than it is for an army of overwhelming strength to fight and thereby achieve its inevitable victory. That answer to the stated question raises problems about the relations among human rationality, human freedom, probability and predictability, which I proceed to explore. Because non-Marxist politicians often devote a lot of energy to the attempt to realize goals that they say are bound to come, the problems have a general interest that transcends the parochial Marxist context in which they most famously arise.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{blackledge2006kautsky,
  author = {Blackledge, Paul},
  title = {Karl Kautsky and Marxist Historiography},
  journal = {Science \& Society: A Journal of Marxist Thought and Analysis},
  year = {2006},
  volume = {70},
  number = {3},
  pages = {337--359},
  doi = {10.1521/siso.70.3.337},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Challenges the "dismissive characterization" of Kautsky as a mechanical or Darwinian evolutionist. Blackledge argues that Kautsky's historical studies — of early Christianity, the Reformation, and contemporary social formations — reveal a sophisticated theory of history that is neither simple economic determinism nor voluntarism, particularly in the period before 1910. The paper rehabilitates Kautsky's contribution to historical materialism while acknowledging persistent political weaknesses.

  RELEVANCE: The most directly relevant secondary source on Kautsky's theory of historical necessity and its philosophical character. Provides detailed engagement with how Kautsky handled the determinism/agency problem in practice, and situates his historiographical approach within broader debates about what kind of "laws" govern history.

  POSITION: Revisionist defense of Kautsky's historical theory against the "mechanism" caricature.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, historical-materialism, determinism, agency, historiography, High},
  abstract = {The traditional dismissive characterization of Karl Kautsky's Marxism as a form of mechanical or even Darwinian evolutionism is a caricature that obscures the very real and important contribution he made to historical materialism in the early years of the last century. Kautsky's historical studies of both the early Church and Reformation “communism,” as well as his attempts to analyze the contemporary Russian and American social formations, show that, while Kautsky's Marxism suffered from consistent political weaknesses, his theory of history, especially as it was articulated in the years before 1910, was sophisticated and powerful. While the power of Kautsky's Marxism became muted after this period, the weaknesses of his later works should not be allowed to obscure the insights contained within his earlier work; insights that informed some of the best work of the next generation of Marxists.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{honderich1982against,
  author = {Honderich, Ted},
  title = {Against teleological historical materialism},
  journal = {Inquiry},
  year = {1982},
  volume = {25},
  number = {4},
  pages = {451--469},
  doi = {10.1080/00201748208601980},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Honderich argues that so-called teleological explanations in historical materialism (where present structures are explained by their future consequences) are in fact disguised causal claims. He contends that teleological interpretations of Marx conflict with the core materialist thesis that history is independent of human consciousness and will, and finds no textual evidence that Marx himself intended a teleological reading.

  RELEVANCE: Directly relevant to assessing whether Kautsky's version of historical materialism involves teleology in a philosophically problematic sense. The "inevitability of socialism" claim in Kautsky is precisely the kind of forward-looking necessity Honderich scrutinizes. His analytic framework helps distinguish legitimate causal-law claims from covert teleology.

  POSITION: Anti-teleological reading of historical materialism; naturalist causal-law interpretation.
  },
  keywords = {teleology, historical-materialism, determinism, Kautsky, Marxism, High},
  abstract = {Marx may be taken to hold that productive forces (e.g. the steam engine) explain productive relations (e.g. capitalism) more than the other way on, and that productive relations explain superstructures (e.g. the legal system) more than the other way on. There are no satisfactory standard causal understandings of these claims about explanatory primacy. That is, no standard causal understanding saves Marx from the traditional objection that relations very greatly affect forces, and superstructures very greatly affect relations. One satisfactorily articulated attempt to save Marx has been the attempt to understand the claims teleologically. Three such understandings can be distinguished, but they do not work. The first fails since it attempts to explain events by way of abstract objects. The second fails since it attempts to explain a thing by means of that thing. The third fails for a related reason. Each understanding also fails for another reason as fundamental. So‐called teleological explanations are in fact claims that standard causal explanations exist, which relevant explanations conflict with the ruling idea of Marx's philosophy, that history is somehow independent of men's consciousness and wills. There may be no evidence that Marx himself intended historical materialism to be understood teleologically. There may be evidence against.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{truper2015historical,
  editor = {Tr{\"u}per, Henning and Chakrabarty, Dipesh and Subrahmanyam, Sanjay},
  title = {Historical Teleologies in the Modern World},
  year = {2015},
  publisher = {Bloomsbury Publishing Plc},
  doi = {10.5040/9781474221108},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: An edited volume tracing how teleological conceptions of history — the view that history is goal-directed — proliferated, fragmented, and competed in Europe and globally throughout the 19th and into the 20th century. The volume situates socialist, nationalist, religious, and scientific teleologies in comparative perspective, tracking how the idea that history "had to" move in a certain direction structured political and intellectual life.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the broadest intellectual-historical context for Kautsky's conviction that socialism is the inevitable endpoint of historical development. Situating Kautsky within the wider culture of historical teleology in the late 19th century shows that his "scientific" claims about necessity were shaped by a general epistemic culture that naturalized teleological thinking. Also bears on the evolutionary Darwinian frameworks Kautsky employed.

  POSITION: Historical and comparative survey of teleological thinking; pluralist, non-Marxist framework.
  },
  keywords = {teleology, philosophy-of-history, Marxism, inevitability, Second-International, Medium},
  abstract = {<JATS1:p>Historical Teleologies in the Modern World tracks the fragmentation and proliferation of teleological understandings of history – the notion that history had to be explained as a goal-directed process – in Europe and beyond throughout the 19th and into the 20th century. Historical teleologies have profoundly informed a variety of other disciplines, including modern philosophy, natural history, literature, humanitarian and religious philanthropism, the political thought and practice of revolution, emancipation, imperialism, colonialism and anti-colonialism, the conceptualization of universal humankind, and the understanding of modernity in general.</JATS1:p> <JATS1:p>By exploring the extension and plurality of historical teleology, the essays in this volume revise the history of historicity in the modern period. Historical Teleologies in the Modern World casts doubt on the idea that a single, if powerful, conception of time could function as the unifying principle of all modern historicity, instead pursuing an investigation of the plurality of modern historicities and its underlying structures. By bringing together Western and non-Western histories, this book provides the first extended treatment of the idea of historical teleology. It will be of great value to students and scholars of modern global and intellectual history.</JATS1:p>},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{bernstein1993preconditions,
  author = {Bernstein, Eduard},
  editor = {Tudor, Henry},
  title = {Eduard Bernstein: The Preconditions of Socialism},
  year = {1993},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9780511802584},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Bernstein's revisionist critique of orthodox Marxism (originally 1899) argues that Marx's economic predictions have not been borne out, that historical materialism is an inadequate guide because it is excessively deterministic, and that socialism must be grounded in Kantian ethics rather than scientific laws of development. Bernstein's neo-Kantian turn introduces a normative "ought" that cannot be derived from empirical historical laws, directly challenging Kautsky's claim that socialist inevitability provides sufficient motivation for political action.

  RELEVANCE: The primary text of the revisionist challenge to Kautsky's philosophy of history. Bernstein's insistence on the is/ought distinction and the need for independent ethical foundations for socialism is the central neo-Kantian challenge that Kautsky had to answer in "Ethics and the Materialist Conception of History" (1906). Essential for understanding the philosophical debate.

  POSITION: Neo-Kantian revisionism; ethical socialism as distinct from scientific determinism.
  },
  keywords = {Bernstein, revisionism, neo-Kantianism, determinism, socialism, ethics, High},
  abstract = {This 1993 book was the first complete new translation of Bernstein's famous and influential work. It will provide students with an accurate and unabridged edition of the classic defence of democratic socialism and the first significant critique of revolutionary Marxism from within the socialist movement. First published in 1899, at the height of the Revisionist Debate, it argued that capitalism was not heading for the major crisis predicted by Marx, and that socialism could be achieved by piece-meal reform within a democratic constitutional framework. Bernstein's work is the focal point of one of the most important political debates of modern times, and crucial for the light it casts on 'the crisis of Communism'. The introduction sites Bernstein's work in its historical and intellectual context, and this edition also provides students with all the necessary reference material for understanding this important text.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{widmer2023left,
  author = {Widmer, Elisabeth Theresia},
  title = {Left-{Kantianism} in the Marburg School},
  year = {2023},
  publisher = {De Gruyter},
  doi = {10.1515/9783111331843},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Widmer analyzes the variety of socialist appropriations of Kant within the Marburg school (Friedrich Albert Lange, Hermann Cohen, Karl Vorländer, Paul Natorp), arguing against the view that this was a coherent unified position. Instead, she identifies "left-Kantianism" as an open, contested cluster of approaches that differed significantly on the relationship between scientific knowledge and ethical normativity, and on how to relate Kant's philosophy to Marxist theory.

  RELEVANCE: The Marburg school's left-Kantians were direct interlocutors in the debate over whether Kautsky's scientific socialism could provide an adequate ethical foundation, or whether a neo-Kantian supplement was required. Widmer's fine-grained account shows the actual diversity of the challenge Kautsky faced, which was not a single unified neo-Kantian position but a spectrum of views.

  POSITION: Historical reconstruction of the Marburg school's engagements with socialism; pluralist rather than pro-Kautsky.
  },
  keywords = {neo-Kantianism, Marburg-school, socialism, ethics, determinism, Second-International, High},
  abstract = {Widmer sheds light on a neglected aspect of the Western philosophical tradition. Following an era of Hegelianism, the members of the neo-Kantian "Marburg School," such as Friedrich Albert Lange, Hermann Cohen, Rudolf Stammler, Paul Natorp, and Ernst Cassirer defended socialism or left-wing ideals on Kantian principles. In doing so, Widmer breaks with two mistaken assumptions. First, Widmer demonstrates that the left-Hegelian and Marxist traditions were not the only significant philosophical sources of socialist critique in nineteenth-century Germany, as the left-Kantians identified problems of normativity that the left-Hegelians could not adequately address. Second, Widmer challenges the prevailing assumption that the political philosophies developed in the Marburg School can be comprehensively characterized as a unified school of "ethical socialism." By showing that they varied fundamentally regarding their political views and their philosophical foundations of socialism, Widmer fills a gap in the studies of neo-Kantianism that is long overdue.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{beiser2009normativity,
  author = {Beiser, Frederick C.},
  title = {Normativity in {Neo-Kantianism}: Its Rise and Fall},
  journal = {International Journal of Philosophical Studies},
  year = {2009},
  volume = {17},
  number = {1},
  pages = {9--27},
  doi = {10.1080/09672550802610941},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Beiser traces how the Southwestern Neo-Kantian school (Windelband, Rickert, Lask) made normativity — the distinction between facts and values, the "is" and the "ought" — central to epistemology, ethics, and the interpretation of German idealism. He traces both the rise and the eventual philosophical difficulties of this normativity concept.

  RELEVANCE: The normativity concept was the key philosophical weapon that neo-Kantian critics of Kautsky deployed: if facts and values are categorically distinct, then no historical-scientific account of capitalism's trajectory can by itself justify socialism as a goal. This article provides the philosophical background for understanding why the neo-Kantian challenge had such force, and what Kautsky was contending with.

  POSITION: Historical reconstruction and analysis of the normativity concept in Neo-Kantianism.
  },
  keywords = {neo-Kantianism, normativity, ethics, Marburg-school, Southwestern-school, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract This article discusses the historical background to the concept of normativity which has a wide use in contemporary philosophy. It locates the origin of that concept in the Southwestern Neo‐Kantian school, the writings of Windelband, Rickert and Lask. The Southwestern school made the concept of normativity central to epistemology, ethics and the interpretation of German idealism. It was their solution to the threats of psycologism and historicism. However, Windelband, Rickert and Lask found difficulties with the concept which eventually forced them to abandon it. These difficulties might be of interest to contemporary philosophers who find the concept of normativity appealing.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{widmer2024cohen,
  author = {Widmer, Elisabeth Theresia},
  title = {Hermann {Cohen's} {Neo-Kantian} Ethical Socialism},
  journal = {Kantian Review},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {29},
  number = {4},
  pages = {607--628},
  doi = {10.1017/s1369415424000396},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Widmer examines Hermann Cohen's ethical socialism — the view that Kantian ethics provides an independent foundation for democratic socialism that does not depend on materialist historical laws. Cohen distinguishes scientific knowledge of historical development from the normative demand for a just social order, arguing that the latter requires a Kantian grounding in universal rational autonomy.

  RELEVANCE: Cohen's position represents the strongest philosophical formulation of the challenge to Kautsky from within neo-Kantianism. Where Bernstein was primarily a political economist, Cohen engages at the level of systematic philosophy, showing why the gap between "is" and "ought" cannot be bridged by any science of history — including Kautsky's materialist conception.

  POSITION: Defense of Cohen's neo-Kantian ethical socialism; critical of purely scientific socialism.
  },
  keywords = {Cohen, neo-Kantianism, ethical-socialism, normativity, Marburg-school, Medium},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
 Hermann Cohen, the founding father of Marburg neo-Kantianism, is known for criticising capitalism from a Kantian ethical perspective. Thus far, the role of the notion of humanity in this critique has been viewed as grounding what I shall call the ‘purposive labour reading’. This reading takes Cohen’s primary interest to lie in a reorganisation of work so that our humanity, which requires us to be treated as ends, remains intact. With the aim to better understand the relevant notion of humanity, I contextualise the discussion within the overall framework of Cohens’ neo-Kantian account of ethical cognition and situate his ideas in the context of his contemporary interlocutors. Revisiting Cohen’s remarks on socialism and capitalism against this backdrop reveals that his discussion of labour serves as an exemplar, showcasing how ethical rationality manifests in the liberal socialists’ demands. I argue that his primary aim was not to prepare the ground for a prescriptive labour theory – though this is likely to follow – but to argue for a framework alternative to historical materialism, allowing us to perceive and interpret social practices in an ethical light.},
}

@book{cohen_berlin1955historical,
  author = {Berlin, Isaiah},
  title = {Historical Inevitability},
  year = {1955},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Berlin's Auguste Comte Memorial Lecture attacks the doctrine of historical inevitability — the view that human history follows a necessary pattern determined by economic, psychological, or cosmic forces. He argues that this doctrine, shared by Marxists and other historicists, undermines moral responsibility and the grounds for praising or blaming historical actors, because it treats human choices as determined outputs of impersonal laws.

  RELEVANCE: The clearest liberal-pluralist attack on the type of historical determinism that underpins Kautsky's system. Berlin's target includes the Marxist tradition's claim to "scientific" knowledge of history's necessary trajectory. His arguments provide a foil for understanding what Kautsky's defenders must answer, and why the "inevitability" claim is philosophically loaded.

  POSITION: Liberal-pluralist anti-determinism; critique of historical inevitability on grounds of moral responsibility.
  },
  keywords = {Berlin, historical-inevitability, determinism, free-will, agency, Medium},
  abstract = {GUL copy owned by Charles E. Wyzanski, Jr. (1906-1986), U.S. District Judge (D. Mass) (1941-1986); GUL copy annotated by Judge WyzanskiDelivered on 12 May 1953 at the London School of Economics and Political Scienc},
  abstract_source = {core},
}

@article{suchting1972marx,
  author = {Suchting, W. A.},
  title = {Marx, {Popper}, and `{Historicism}'},
  journal = {Inquiry},
  year = {1972},
  volume = {15},
  number = {1-4},
  pages = {235--266},
  doi = {10.1080/00201747208601662},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Suchting distinguishes two types of laws of historical development (universal laws of succession vs. tendential laws) and argues that Popper's arguments against historicism fail against both. He then offers a careful analysis of four models of historical determinism and asks which is most plausible to ascribe to Marx, concluding that Marx's commitment to historical laws is more nuanced than either Popperian critics or orthodox defenders acknowledge.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a conceptual toolkit for distinguishing what kind of historical "laws" or "necessities" Kautsky invokes: are they universal, tendential, probabilistic, or teleological? This analytic precision is essential for assessing whether Kautsky's philosophy of history falls to Popperian critiques and what philosophical resources it has.

  POSITION: Analytic defense of Marx's historical laws against Popperian historicism critique.
  },
  keywords = {historical-laws, determinism, Popper, historicism, Marxism, Medium},
  abstract = {According to Sir Karl Popper, there is a harmful approach to the social sciences called ‘historicism’. This takes their principal aim to be historical prediction of an unconditional sort and the chief means to this the discovery of laws of historical development. The chief exemplar is held to be Marx. This paper distinguishes two possible sorts of laws of historical development. Popper's arguments against each are rejected. Which sort it is most plausible to ascribe to Marx is considered. Four models of historical determinism are distinguished and the question of Marx's view or views on the question is discussed in terms of them.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{gronow2016formation,
  author = {Gronow, Jukka},
  title = {On the Formation of {Marxism}},
  year = {2016},
  publisher = {BRILL},
  doi = {10.1163/9789004306653},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Gronow analyzes the formation of Marxism in Kautsky's theoretical system, situating Kautsky's theory of capitalism and historical development within the intellectual context of the Second International. The book examines how Kautsky synthesized Darwinian evolutionary theory, Marxist political economy, and scientific naturalism into a distinctive system, and how this system relates to Marx's own critique of political economy.

  RELEVANCE: The most recent comprehensive scholarly treatment of Kautsky's theoretical formation, directly addressing his philosophy of history, his economic determinism, and his relationship to Marx. Essential for understanding the intellectual sources and internal structure of Kautsky's system of historical necessity and evolutionary inevitability.

  POSITION: Sympathetic scholarly reconstruction of Kautsky's theoretical development; situates him in the Second International.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, formation-of-Marxism, Second-International, determinism, political-economy, High},
  abstract = {Preface and Acknowledgements Introduction PART I: KAUTSKY'S MARXISM 1. Organised Capitalism, the General Cartel and the Proletariat 2. The Dispute over Revisionism 3. The Theory of Immiseration, Socialist Consciousness and the Intellectuals 4. Socialism as Science 5. The Capitalist Law of Appropriation: Kautsky's Interpretation of Karl Marx's Economic Thought 6. The Centralisation of Capital and Monopoly Formation 7. Imperialism and the Relation between Industrial and Agrarian Countries 8. Imperialism and Its Alternatives 9. Imperialism as the Last Stage of Capitalism 10. Theoretical Sources of Kautsky's and Lenin's Studies on Imperialism 11. Imperialism as the Truth about Capitalism 12. Parliamentary Democracy and Revolutionary Tactics 13. The Question of Democracy and Dictatorship: Lenin's Critique of Kautsky the Renegade PART II: MARX'S MARXISM 14. The Immanent Critique and the Natural Rights Theory 15. John Locke, Adam Smith and Karl Marx's Critique of Private Property 16. The Principle of Labour 17. The Theory of Increasing Misery and the Critique of Capitalism Conclusion Bibliography Index},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{steger1997quest,
  author = {Steger, Manfred B.},
  title = {The Quest for Evolutionary Socialism},
  year = {1997},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9780511558603},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Steger provides a comprehensive intellectual biography of Eduard Bernstein's revisionism, tracing how his encounter with empirical evidence and neo-Kantian philosophy led him to reject economic determinism, class-struggle orthodoxy, and revolutionary Marxism in favor of gradual, ethically grounded social reform. The book carefully reconstructs the revisionist debate with Kautsky, Luxemburg, and others.

  RELEVANCE: The most thorough treatment of the revisionist challenge to Second International orthodoxy, which was primarily a challenge to Kautsky's determinism and its ethical implications. Steger shows precisely how Bernstein's neo-Kantian turn was a response to the perceived philosophical inadequacy of Kautsky's historical materialism.

  POSITION: Intellectual history of revisionism; broadly sympathetic to Bernstein's philosophical concerns.
  },
  keywords = {Bernstein, revisionism, neo-Kantianism, evolutionary-socialism, determinism, Second-International, Medium},
  abstract = {The Quest for Evolutionary Socialism uses Eduard Bernstein's life and works as the basis for an examination of the interactions between European social democratic politics and socialist political ideas. It is thus a timely response to the need for a new, comprehensive biography of Bernstein, the German 'Father of Marxist Revisionism'. Professor Steger incorporates recent academic developments and addresses current debates on the 'End of Socialism' resulting from the collapse of Marxism-Leninism and the chronic ailments of European social democracy. This study is set within the historical context of the European labour movement and thus Steger interprets Bernstein's 'Evolutionary Socialism' as an ethically motivated quest for liberty, solidarity and distributive justice. Steger stresses that the future of social democracy depends on its ability to heed Bernstein's call for critical self-reflection and to reorientate towards more liberal ideals},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{lih2006lenin,
  author = {Lih, Lars T.},
  title = {Lenin Rediscovered},
  year = {2006},
  publisher = {BRILL},
  doi = {10.1163/9789047417873},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lih's major scholarly work argues that Lenin's "What Is to Be Done?" has been systematically misread; properly understood, it is a Kautskyite document that reproduces Kautsky's own argument about the relationship between the socialist intelligentsia and the working class. Lih extensively reconstructs Kautsky's "erfurtian synthesis" — the unified system of historical inevitability, party organization, and socialist consciousness.

  RELEVANCE: While primarily about Lenin, this book provides the most detailed reconstruction of what Lih calls Kautsky's "heroic Marxism" and its theory of historical necessity: socialism is inevitable, the movement is destined to succeed, and the task of the party is to make workers conscious of what history demands of them. Lih shows how Kautsky's determinism generates rather than undermines political will.

  POSITION: Revisionist rehabilitation of Kautsky's orthodoxy as the dominant framework of Second International Marxism.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, Lenin, Second-International, historical-necessity, erfurtian-synthesis, agency, Medium},
  abstract = {Lenin’s What is to Be Done? (1902) has long been seen as the founding document of a 'party of a new type'. For some, it provided a model of ‘vanguard party’ that was the essence of Bolshevism, for others it manifested Lenin’s élitist and manipulatory attitude towards the workers. This substantial new commentary, based on contemporary Russian- and German-language sources, provides hitherto unavailable contextual information that undermines these views and shows how Lenin's argument rests squarely on an optimistic confidence in the workers' revolutionary inclinations and on his admiration of German Social Democracy in particular. Lenin's outlook cannot be understood, Lih claims here, outside the context of international Social Democracy, the disputes within Russian Social Democracy and the institutions of the revolutionary underground. The new translation focuses attention on hard-to-translate key terms. This study raises new and unsettling questions about the legacy of Marx, Bolshevism as a historical force, and the course of Soviet history, but, most of all, it will revolutionise the conventional interpretations of Lenin.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{rowley2024bogdanov,
  author = {Rowley, David G.},
  title = {Marxism as a Natural Science: {Alexander Bogdanov's} Anti-Revisionist Revisionism},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {32},
  number = {2},
  pages = {265--294},
  doi = {10.1163/1569206x-bja10035},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Rowley examines Alexander Bogdanov's attempt to reconstruct Marxism on a natural-scientific basis, placing it in the context of both revisionism and the effort to "scientize" historical materialism. Bogdanov accepted the challenge of making Marxism philosophically rigorous while rejecting neo-Kantian solutions, seeking instead an empirio-monist grounding that he believed was compatible with revolutionary commitment.

  RELEVANCE: Bogdanov's project ran parallel to Kautsky's: both sought to ground socialism in a naturalistic, scientific worldview and both had to confront the neo-Kantian challenge. Bogdanov's case illuminates the broader problem of grounding historical necessity without a teleological metaphysics, and his "anti-revisionist revisionism" highlights the difficulty that any scientistic Marxism faces in preserving the link between historical laws and political action.

  POSITION: Reconstructive analysis of Bogdanov's philosophy; situated within the Second International debate about scientific socialism.
  },
  keywords = {Bogdanov, Second-International, natural-science, Marxism, determinism, revisionism, Low},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
Discussion of Alexander Bogdanov as a Marxist revisionist has largely centred on his philosophy of being and cognition and on Plekhanov’s and Lenin’s accusation that Bogdanov was an idealist renegade from Marxism. However, the real issue of revisionism at the time was not materialism but determinism: the question of whether socialism would appear by the working of the objective laws of nature or the subjective will of human beings. Bogdanov did indeed revise Marxism, but he did so in order to defend it as a natural science. He conceived of society as a form of life subject to biological laws of nature: the need to assimilate energy from the environment and the need to adapt to the environment in the struggle to survive. He reinterpreted the key elements of scientific socialism – the idea that existence determines consciousness and the labour theory of value – in terms of this worldview.},
}

@article{blackledge2006trotsky,
  author = {Blackledge, Paul},
  title = {{Leon Trotsky's} Contribution to the {Marxist} Theory of History},
  journal = {Studies in East European Thought},
  year = {2006},
  volume = {58},
  number = {1},
  pages = {1--31},
  doi = {10.1007/s11212-005-3677-z},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Blackledge argues that Trotsky's theory of combined and uneven development and his treatment of individual agency in history represent both a continuation of and a departure from Second International Marxism, including Kautsky's framework. Against critics who see Trotsky as simply "voluntarist," Blackledge shows he drew on the strengths of Kautsky's determinism while transcending its fatalistic weaknesses.

  RELEVANCE: The Trotsky/Kautsky comparison is directly illuminating for assessing Kautsky's handling of agency within a determinist framework. Blackledge's analysis shows what distinguishing a sophisticated from a mechanical determinism looks like in practice, and locates Kautsky's theory in relation to the range of positions in Second International Marxism.

  POSITION: Sympathetic reconstruction of Trotsky's theory; contextualizes Kautsky's determinism via comparison.
  },
  keywords = {Trotsky, Kautsky, Second-International, historical-theory, agency, determinism, Low, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}
